.DS_Store
.pio
.vscode/.browse.c_cpp.db*
.vscode/c_cpp_properties.json
.vscode/launch.json
.vscode/ipch
data/
firmware/src_appliance/fw_version.h
firmware/src_config/fw_version.h
firmware/src_display/fw_version.h
firmware/src_faucet/fw_version.h
firmware/src_front/fw_version.h
firmware/src_pcba_bench/fw_version.h
firmware/src_prototype/fw_version.h
firmware/src_appliance/fw_build_time.h
firmware/src_prototype/fw_build_time.h
**/xcuserdata/
**/build/
__pycache__/
*.xcodeproj/
logs/*.log
.claude/*
!.claude/commands/
node_modules

# CAD script output (generated STEP/STL files)

# board-3d.py: the component-model download cache (manufacturer STEPs keyed by
# LCSC) and the on-demand full B-rep assembly are regenerable and large. The
# committed out/<board>.glb is the viewer artifact.
hardware/pcb/pcba/.cad-cache/
hardware/pcb/pcba/out/*.step

# Atomic-export temp files: hardware/_cadq_export.py writes a
# `.NAME.EXT.RANDOM.EXT` sibling then renames it atomically onto the target.
# A hard-killed run (SIGKILL/SIGTERM mid-write) can orphan one — never commit it.
.*.step.*.step
.*.stl.*.stl
.*.dxf.*.dxf
.*.pdf.*.pdf

# Remotion's lock file — installed by hand on one machine, and nothing else reads it.
# `tools/render`'s is NOT here: the derive workflow installs those renderers too, and what
# they draw is committed. An unpinned puppeteer renders the same card to different pixels,
# which is a diff on every run in files the tree holds.
marketing/video/remotion/package-lock.json

# Remotion channel foundation: render outputs and caches are regenerable
marketing/video/remotion/out/
marketing/video/remotion/.remotion/
.firebase-setup/
tools/flavor_preview.png

# Local CadQuery venv (tools/cad-venv/bin/python) — regenerable, machine-specific.
# No trailing slash: a checkout that links the toolchain in rather than installing it
# has a symlink here, and a trailing slash matches only a directory.
tools/cad-venv

# Tessellation written beside each STEP for the viewer to read instead of parsing it
# (hardware/scripts/_cadq_export.py). Rebuilt by every export; the page parses the STEP
# when one is absent.
*.step.mesh

# The solids arrive in a release asset. hardware/cad-artifacts.lock.json names that asset and
# every solid in it by sha256, tools/cad-artifacts/pack.py builds and pins one, and
# web/scripts/fetch-cad-artifacts.mjs puts them on the disk a deploy serves from. The lock is
# what git carries.
*.step
# Except the harvested solids, which have no builder here — y_divider.py:3 says it of its own,
# and manifold_layout.py places the elbow and the tee. `pack.py`'s HARVESTED names the same three.
!hardware/reference/elbow-connector/elbow-connector.step
!hardware/reference/tee-connector/tee-connector.step
!hardware/reference/y-divider/y-divider.step

# Solids drawn once and kept (hardware/scripts/_realized.py). A miss costs what the build
# always cost; deleting this is always safe.
.cache/

# The seconds each generator's last runs took, on the machine that ran them
# (hardware/scripts/_build_time.py). What ledger/build-time.md carries is the figure these
# settle on; a checkout that has run nothing has no file here and moves no figure.
hardware/ledger/.build-time.samples.json
hardware/ledger/.build-time.samples.json.tmp

# Scene STEPs are a rendering intermediate for the unit cards' pictures — a cut
# of enclosure-assembly.step, regenerated on demand by
# hardware/assembly/scenes/render_scenes.py. What is committed is the PNG in
# cards/img/ and the fingerprint beside it; the STEPs would churn on every move
# of any body they contain, which is exactly the commit cost the pictures must
# not add.
hardware/assembly/scenes/out/
bazel-*

# Where this checkout is, which is not where any other checkout is. `gen_build.py` writes it
# and `.bazelrc` takes it with `try-import`, so BUILD.bazel carries no machine's path.
.bazelrc.paths

# The printed deck and its cards, drawn from the HTML by a browser
# (hardware/assembly/cards/_build.py). Every card build rewrites the whole set, and a PDF does
# not delta-compress, so each one that landed added another entire 20-40 MB blob — eight copies
# of deck.pdf are the largest objects in this repo's pack, ahead of every solid and every model.
# Nothing here reads them back: `//:cards-build` names all 104 in `outs` and not one in `srcs`,
# no web route serves them, and no doc embeds one. So the build makes them, sync_tree lands them
# on the disk, and nothing carries them into a commit.
hardware/assembly/cards/out/
